Atlas: stop the dirty-tree rebuild storm (root cause of the 120 s discovery timeouts)

Status: NOT APPLIED — produced by shift 6 of software_developer, 2026-11-19.
Apply with:  git apply docs/patches/003-atlas-dirty-tree-rebuild-storm.patch

MEASURED CHAIN (card d8534b12)
1. `project_changes(scope="all")` in this very session: **172 files** dirty vs HEAD
   (the `.codx` control plane alone accounts for most of them: `.codx/autonomy/state.json`,
   `.codx/harness/audit.jsonl` (+1084 lines during one shift), `.codx/chat_turns/*.json`,
   `.codx/tasks/**/*.json`).
2. `codx/junior/atlas/indexer.py:46` — `MAX_INCREMENTAL_FILES = 40`.
3. `codx/junior/atlas/indexer.py:218-229` — any dirty set larger than that cap does not
   patch the index, it calls `_build_full()`.
4. `project_health()` reports the cost of one `_build_full()`: **built in 36.01 s**
   (671 files, 10185 symbols, 21423 edges, 25.8 MB db).
5. `constants.py:42` `DEFAULT_TTL_SECONDS = 5` and `query.py:164`
   `getattr(settings, "project_atlas_ttl_seconds", 5)` make the freshness proof
   (indexer.py:206) expire after 5 s, so consecutive discovery calls each re-run
   `git status --porcelain` + two `git ls-files` (indexer.py:218-219).
6. `indexer.py:1088-1098` `_watermark()` = HEAD + `.git/index` identity, i.e. **content
   changes that are not staged do not change it**. So the fast path (indexer.py:198-204)
   can also serve a stale index for up to `scan_seconds` (60 s) and then pay the rebuild
   in whichever call lands after the window.
7. Result: `project_locate` exceeded the 120 s tool cap **in this session**
   (reproduced: `project_locate(query="shared_atlas", limit=2)` -> "timed out after 120s").
   `project_health` survives only because it is a single `ensure()` returning stats
   (~36-40 s), just under the cap.

WHY IT HITS AUTONOMOUS SHIFTS HARDER
Every card comment, chat turn and audit line rewrites **tracked** `.codx` files, so the
dirty set can never fall back under 40 while an actor is working. The atlas stays in a
rebuild loop for the whole shift.

HUNKS
Hunk 1 (the fix that matters): raise the incremental cap so the observed 172-file dirty
  tree patches instead of rebuilding. 400 < the 5000-file index cap, so nothing overflows.
Hunk 2 + 3: give the freshness proof a realistic lifetime (5 s -> 30 s) so a burst of
  discovery calls in one turn shares one scan.
Hunk 4 (optional): expose `project_atlas_scan_seconds`. `query.py:165-168` already reads
  it, but `CODXJuniorSettings` (settings.py:143-154) defines only `project_atlas_enabled`,
  `project_atlas_max_files` and `project_atlas_ttl_seconds`, so today the attribute never
  exists and the window is pinned at `DEFAULT_SCAN_SECONDS = 60`.

TRADE-OFF / KNOWN GAP
Raising the cap makes the first scan cheap; it does not bound a real mass change (branch
switch, generated tree) — that still rebuilds, correctly. Because `_watermark()` ignores
unstaged content, a larger `scan_seconds` also means a longer window in which newly
written files are not yet visible to discovery; 60 s (unchanged default) is the existing
behaviour, so hunk 4 does not widen it unless an operator sets it.

ZERO-CODE OVERRIDE (partial, no patch needed)
`query.py:163-168` reads from project settings, and `settings.py:151-154` really does
define `project_atlas_ttl_seconds`, so an operator can raise **that one** knob (e.g. 60)
without patching. The scan window is NOT configurable until hunk 4 lands. Cost of the
override: the index may be up to TTL seconds stale.

NOT VERIFIED FROM THIS SHIFT
How many `_build_full()` calls one `project_locate` triggers (no shell/profiler tool). If
it is one, the timeout is simply the rebuild being slower than the 36 s recorded in the
health meta (the index has grown since); if it is several, hunks 2-3 are what remove the
